WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:05.600
Let’s say you really need
to find reliable information

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.880
about the best diet for
high blood pressure,

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.960
or heart disease,
or diabetes.

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:12.400
Where do you go?

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.880
Do you go to a website
sponsored by Big Pharma

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.680
that wants to sell you
pills to fix your problem?

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.800
Or, do you want
to treat the cause?

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.240
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:27.760
with the latest peer-
reviewed research

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.560
on the best ways to eat
healthy and live longer.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.200
Today we bring you some important
research on processed meat,

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.880
which has been classified
as a Group 1 carcinogen.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.760
How did the meat
industry, government,

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.040
and cancer organizations
respond to that news?

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:46.880
Here’s our first story.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.920
“It is [perhaps] rare, in
the history of nations,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.200
that one finds good reasons
to render homage to the

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.240
generosity and altruism of
governments and those in power,

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.560
but the birth of the International
Agency for Research on Cancer

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.280
(IARC) presents one
of those rare occasions.”

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.080
It all started with a single
letter from a bereaved husband,

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.840
relating the suffering of his
wife after a cancer diagnosis,

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.280
cascading into this open
letter calling for governments

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.040
to devote half of 1 percent
of their military budgets

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.280
to fight for life, by attacking
one of the greatest plagues

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:22.560
that weigh on humanity.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.360
And 18 months later,
the IARC was born

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.280
in the World Health Organization.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.280
With what overarching motive?
Cancer prevention.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.160
The IARC is best known for its
monographs, book-sized reports

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.360
evaluating whether or not
some suspected carcinogen does

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.200
in fact cause cancer.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.800
They are generally accepted
as close to a ﬁnal word

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.080
as there is on whether or
not something is carcinogenic.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.040
And their 114th monograph,
published in 2018, was on meat.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.160
After thoroughly reviewing the
accumulated scientific literature,

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.960
a Working Group of 22
experts from 10 countries,

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.360
after considering more
than 800 different studies,

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.720
concluded their 500-page
report by establishing that

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.560
something like a burger or pork
chop is probably carcinogenic,

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.240
probably causes cancer.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.040
But processed meat was
placed as a Group 1 carcinogen,

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.120
the highest level of certainty,
meaning that according

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.040
to the best available
evidence the consumption

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.760
of processed meat causes cancer.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.520
So, that means foods
like bacon cause cancer;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.440
ham, hot dogs, breakfast links,
lunch meat cause cancer.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.440
But their definition also
includes, for example,

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:34.880
turkey deli slices.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:39.600
Specifically, eating processed
meat causes colorectal cancer,

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.120
cancers of the colon or
rectum, the second most deadly

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.880
cancer worldwide, after lung cancer

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.880
which is caused largely from smoking.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.400
Colorectal cancer is the second
leading cause of cancer death

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.080
here in the United States as well,

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.080
and it doesn’t just strike older people.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.640
It’s also a leading cause
of cancer and death

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.960
from cancer earlier in life as well.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.280
The meat industry wasn’t
happy, calling it

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.440
a “dramatic and alarmist overreach.”

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.200
Speaking of dramatic and
alarmist overreach,

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.920
one ag group in Italy
sent out a press release:

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.720
Just say no to terrorism on meat.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.600
The gloves were off.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.320
The meat industry in Canada
tried to pressure the government

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:24.000
to cut off funds to the IARC,
asking the Health Minister

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.760
to pull all funding from the agency

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.560
after they dared to question meat.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.000
And the U.S. meat industry
did the same thing.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.560
It’s no surprise the IARC is
under siege by corporate interests,

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.640
trying to challenge
their cancer evaluations

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.240
on Monsanto’s Roundup
pesticide and meat;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.840
trying to discredit the agency
and undermine financial support.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.360
Internal documents have revealed
that Monsanto scientists,

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.160
for example, “casually
discussing ‘ghost-writing’

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.960
scientific papers and
suppressing any science that

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.520
conflicts with the company’s
assertions of safety.”

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.520
The chemical industry has
joined the corporate cacophony,

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.480
calling the IARC monographs
“dubious and misleading.”

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:07.360
These are classic strategies
straight out of the

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.120
tobacco industry playbook.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.600
“But there is little to suggest
that, as a corporate actor,

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.840
’Big Tobacco‘ differs
fundamentally from

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.240
’Big Booze’ or ’Big Food’", for example.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.560
One recurring corporate talking
point is that basically

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.920
the IARC never met a
carcinogen it didn’t like.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.480
But the vast majority end up
being categorized as just

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.000
possibly carcinogenic, or there
really aren’t sufficient data

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.840
to make a determination either way.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.240
And look, they only spend
time looking at substances

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.800
for which there is
already an existing body of

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.880
scientific literature
indicating a degree

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.800
of carcinogenic hazard to humans;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.320
so, no wonder many of them
end up, indeed, carcinogenic.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.800
How did the IARC respond
to all the criticism?

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.560
The World Health Organization
received a number of queries,

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.840
expressions of concern,
and requests for clarification

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.880
following the publication of
their meat and cancer report.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.480
They replied, hey we never
told anyone to stop eating

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.800
processed meats—
your body your choice.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.200
They just indicated that
reducing consumption of these

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.160
products can reduce the risk
of a leading cancer killer.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:13.200
So hey, you like cancer? You do you.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:18.160
The IARC is just a research
organization that evaluates

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:19.920
the evidence on the causes of cancer;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:24.000
after that, what you do with
that information is up to you.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.160
The American Cancer
Society was nice

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:27.920
and clear when it came to alcohol.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:30.720
When it comes to cancer, it
is best not to drink alcohol.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.200
But they got a little more
wishy-washy with processed meat,

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:36.720
suggesting people can get away
with just limiting their intake.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.280
The European Commission
was a little clearer.

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:41.360
To reduce our risk of cancer
we should eat plenty of

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:43.360
whole grains, pulses
(which are beans, split peas,

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.000
chickpeas and lentils),
vegetables and fruits,

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.520
limit sugary, fatty, salty
foods, and straight-up avoid

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:51.840
soda, sausage, and
other processed meats.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.160
After all, in answering
the question how much meat

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:58.400
is safe to eat, the IARC
replied that we don’t yet know

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:00.800
whether a safe level exists, period.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:07.440
In this next story, I quantify the
risks of colon and rectal cancers

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.120
from eating foods like bacon,
ham, hot dogs, sausage, and lunch meat.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:15.840
In 2018, arguably the most
prestigious cancer research institution

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:19.113
in the world, the International Agency
for Research on Cancer (IARC),

00:06:19.113 --> 00:06:22.000
within the World Health Organization,
published their report

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.440
on processed meat, concluding
that foods like bacon, ham,

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.120
hot dogs, lunch meat, and
sausages are cancer causing,

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.320
classifying processed meat
as a Group 1 carcinogen.

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:34.720
“These findings,” concluded
the director of the agency,

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:37.120
“further support current
public health recommendations

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:38.880
to limit the intake of meat.”

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:42.560
Critics questioned putting
processed meat in the same

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:46.640
carcinogenic classification
as asbestos or tobacco.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:50.160
Or as a pesticide company
roughly put it, how can eating

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:53.840
processed meat fall into the
same category as mustard gas.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:58.000
The classifications only relate
to the strength of evidence

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.080
that the agent causes cancer
or not, not how much cancer.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:04.560
This does not mean that
they are all equally dangerous.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:08.000
It’s safer to eat a sandwich
filled with pastrami than plutonium,

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.480
even though they are both
Group 1 carcinogens;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.360
both substances known to
cause cancer in people.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:16.480
Okay, so just how
dangerous is meat?

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:21.280
The relative risk of colorectal
cancer was 18 percent

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:25.840
for every 50 grams a day. Okay,
so what exactly does that mean?

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:29.760
Well, 50 grams is about one hot
dog, or two breakfast links,

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:32.160
or two slices of
Canadian bacon or ham.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.880
So, a daily sandwich with one
or two slices of baloney

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:38.080
would increase your colorectal
cancer risk by 18 percent.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.320
But a half-pound
pastrami on rye would

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:42.320
bump it up more like 80 percent.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:47.120
Okay, but what does the 18 percent
increased risk really mean?

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:51.920
One way to look at it is
absolute risk versus relative risk.

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:54.800
Assuming that the lifetime
risk of colorectal cancer is

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.320
about 5 percent (1 in 20),
increasing your risk about

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:00.880
20 percent would only
bump up your absolute risk

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:04.400
of getting colorectal cancer
from 5 percent to 6 percent.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:07.280
Now, on a population scale,
an 18 percent drop in risk

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:11.520
could mean about 25,000 fewer
cases of colorectal cancer

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:15.040
every year in the United States,
25,000 fewer families a year

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:18.960
dealing with that diagnosis,
if we swapped out the daily

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.840
sandwich for hummus or
chose veggie dogs instead.

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:24.800
So, it all depends on
how you look at it.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:28.240
Colorectal cancer is our second
leading cause of cancer death

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:31.200
for men and women combined,
after lung cancer.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:34.160
So, if you don’t smoke,
colon and rectal cancer

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:36.320
may be your greatest cancer nemesis.

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:40.480
But we can drop the risk
of getting it by about a fifth

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:43.600
with a single dietary tweak:
cutting a serving of

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:45.360
processed meat out of our daily diet.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:49.120
How does 18 percent
increased cancer risk

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:51.040
compare to other risky behavior?

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:55.040
In my testimony before the Dietary
Guidelines Scientific Committee,

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.480
I made what may sound
like a hyperbolic metaphor.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:01.040
I asked, “We try not to
smoke around our kids,

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:04.960
why would we send them to
school with a baloney sandwich?”

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:07.200
That is not hyperbole.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:10.000
According to the Surgeon
General, living with a smoker

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.360
increases your risk of
lung cancer by 15 percent.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:16.480
So, breathing second-hand
smoke day in and day out

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:19.920
increases your risk of lung
cancer almost as much as

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.880
eating a serving of processed
meat day in and day out

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.040
increases your risk
of colorectal cancer.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:28.800
The meat industry responded
by saying that the risks

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:31.600
and benefits must be
considered together before

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:33.440
telling people what to eat or breathe.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:36.240
Think about all the baloney benefits.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:39.680
Lunch meat isn’t just about
cancer but convenience.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:43.120
Indeed, processed meat
isn’t just about cancer.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:45.760
An article railing against
the World Health Organization’s

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:49.360
“meat terrorism” cited the
Global Burden of Disease studies

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:52.160
comparing how many cancer deaths
are caused by processed meat

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:55.600
consumption compared to
tobacco or alcohol use.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:57.440
But if you look at the
study they’re referencing,

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:01.120
the 30-something thousand
deaths attributable to higher

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:04.640
processed meat intake are
just the colorectal cancer deaths,

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:08.400
and don’t also include the
100,000 deaths from diabetes

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:11.040
or the 400,000 deaths from heart disease.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.400
So, in actuality, we may be
talking a half million deaths

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.720
attributable to processed meat.

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:18.960
And it’s not just
colon and rectal cancer.

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:22.800
If you look at the science since
the IARC decision was published,

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:24.880
processed meat may also
increase the risk of

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:28.080
prostate cancer, breast cancer,
and pancreatic cancer.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:31.520
Unfortunately, despite growing
public health concerns

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:35.120
about processed meat consumption,
there have been no changes

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.520
in the amount of processed
meat consumed by U.S. adults

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:38.880
over the last 18 years.

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:40.800
Of course, it would
have helped if the last

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:43.360
Dietary Guidelines for Americans
had happened to mention

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:46.080
that processed meat was a carcinogen.

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:49.120
An explicit and science-based
statement on processed meat

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:51.920
in the next Dietary Guidelines
would certainly help.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:55.840
But the scientific committee
made no such recommendation.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:00.640
Sadly, even those diagnosed with
colorectal cancer hardly improve

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:04.480
their overall lifestyle after
diagnosis, though that may be

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:07.840
because “70 percent of cancer
patients had never received

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:09.760
nutrition advice from
their [medical] providers

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:12.720
during or after treatment.”
That just blows me away.

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:16.720
“Despite the continued
obfuscation of the issue

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:20.720
by the meat industry—they learned
well from the tobacco merchants—

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:23.840
meat should continue to be
a focus of public health action.”

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:27.280
New York City is leading
the way, passing legislation

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:29.280
to ban processed meats
from school meals.

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:32.720
What a concept, not giving
our kids carcinogens.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:35.440
Meanwhile, the processed
meat industry

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:37.360
is trying to reformulate its products.

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:39.840
It’s kind of like in
the pharmaceutical area,

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:42.000
where you try to mitigate
the potential adverse effects

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.800
of one drug by prescribing
an additional drug.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:47.680
Like you could add fiber to
hot dogs or something to try

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:51.040
to counterbalance the risk,
potentially reducing the cancer load

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:53.840
by changing how it’s
processed rather than by

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:56.176
banning processed meat altogether.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:00.000
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.280
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:05.840
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:09.600
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:13.280
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:16.560
please go to the Nutrition Facts
Podcast landing page.

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:19.120
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:24.240
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite to each of these topics.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:27.440
For a timely text on the
